theory Day24_Separability
  imports
    Quantum_Computing11
    Vern_Paulsen_QC_3_10_Lmat.Separable_Cone
begin

section \<open>Days 26--28 (28 October -- 2 November 2011): separability and entanglement witnesses\<close>

text \<open>Entanglement revisited.  Ensembles $\{\psi_i,p_i\}$ on $\Hil_A$ and $\{\phi_j,q_j\}$ on
$\Hil_B$ compose to the density matrix
$\sum_{i,j}p_iq_j\ketbra{\psi_i}{\psi_i}\otimes\ketbra{\phi_j}{\phi_j}$.
\begin{notesdef}[Day 26]
A density matrix $P\in L(\Hil_A\otimes\Hil_B)$ is \emph{separable} if
$P=\sum_lp_l\ketbra{\psi_l}{\psi_l}\otimes\ketbra{\phi_l}{\phi_l}$ with unit vectors
$\psi_l\in\Hil_A$, $\phi_l\in\Hil_B$, $p_l\ge0$, $\sum p_l=1$; otherwise $P$ is
\emph{entangled}.  More generally (Day 27) $Sep\subseteq M_r(M_n)$ is the set of matrices
$\sum_lP_l\otimes Q_l$ with $P_l\ge0$, $Q_l\ge0$.
\end{notesdef}
Are there entangled density matrices, and how can one tell?  The notes answer with the
theory of convex cones:
\begin{notestheorem}[Days 27--28]
Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real vector space, $K\subseteq V$ closed and convex, $y\notin K$.
Then there is a linear $f:V\to\R$ and $\alpha\in\R$ with $f(y)<\alpha\le f(K)$; if $C$ is a closed
cone and $w\notin C$ there is $f$ with $f(w)<0\le f(C)$.
\end{notestheorem}
The separable matrices form a closed cone $C$ in the real vector space of Hermitian matrices;
an entangled $R$ is separated from $C$ by a real functional, which complexifies to
$\tilde f(H+iK)=f(H)+if(K)$, and $\tilde f$ corresponds to a Hermitian matrix $W$ with
$\Tr(WR)<0\le\Tr(W\sigma)$ for all separable $\sigma$: an \emph{entanglement witness}.  The
notes phrase the final criterion through positive maps $\Phi_f$ obtained from the Arveson
correspondence; that form is \<open>separable_iff_positive_maps\<close> in
\<open>Day27_Positive_Map_Criterion\<close>, derived from the witness form proved here.

The formal development uses the typed matrices \<open>lmat\<close> so that HOL-Analysis' separation
theorem applies: \<open>separable_cone_lmat\<close> is the convex cone generated by tensor products of
positive matrices and \<open>Sprime\<close> is the set of product density matrices.\<close>

subsection \<open>The separable cone (Day 27)\<close>

text \<open>The cone is convex, conic, contains $0$, and consists of Hermitian matrices.\<close>

subsection \<open>Separable density matrices (Day 26)\<close>

text \<open>For a density matrix $\rho$ (positive with $\Tr\rho=1$), membership in the cone is the
notes' definition: $\rho$ is a convex combination of product density matrices
$\sigma_i\otimes\tau_i$.\<close>

subsection \<open>The separable cone is closed (Day 28)\<close>

text \<open>The notes record that $C$ is a closed cone (needed for the separation theorem); here the
cone is closed in the Hilbert--Schmidt topology and the separable density matrices form a
compact convex set.\<close>

subsection \<open>Separation (Days 27--28)\<close>

text \<open>The separation theorem applied to the cone: a non-separable $\rho$ is separated from $C$
by a real-linear functional (an inner product against some $a$), strictly for density
matrices because the separable density matrices are compact.\<close>

subsection \<open>Entanglement witnesses (Day 28)\<close>

text \<open>An \emph{entanglement witness} for $\rho$ is a Hermitian $W$ with $\Tr(W\sigma)\ge0$ on every
separable $\sigma$ and $\Tr(W\rho)<0$.  (i) Every non-separable Hermitian $\rho$ has a witness;
(ii) density matrices have a witness with a strict margin; (iii) (Horodecki) $\rho$ is
separable iff every Hermitian $W$ that is non-negative on the cone is non-negative on
$\rho$.\<close>

text \<open>This closes the entanglement chapter of the notes.  The Horodecki--Peres criterion for
two qubits is \<open>Day28_Horodecki_Peres\<close>, the positive-map formulation
\<open>Day27_Positive_Map_Criterion\<close>; the universal-witness question (Day 28) is not
formalised.\<close>

end
